"README.md:7: louis-agent is a framework: the engine (the tool loop, streaming, providers, cost tracking and safety guards, built on\ndemos/louis-agent/docs/specs/ORCHESTRATOR_DESIGN.md:48: | 6 | **Implementation tier**: originally Anthropic\u0027s Tool Runner (\u0060BetaToolRunner\u0060, C#), since the orchestrator has no multi-provider requirement and it matches the stack in place (\u0060Axiz.Adobe\u0060 is a .NET/C# repository); its tools (\u0060CallLouisAgentFix\u0060, escalations to other agents) are plain HTTP/MCP calls, not in-process hand-offs | Built on \u0060LouisAgent.Core\u0060 instead (an \u0060AgentEngine\u0060 with its own \u0060toolsets:\u0060), reusing providers, the skills loader and the tool loop with no new dependency | **Decide** which to keep before building the real one |\nsrc/docs/AGENT_COMMUNICATION.md:25: | A program (or another agent) to hand louis-agent a whole task and get the result back | **LouisAgent.Api** sessions | louis-agent runs its own tool loop server-side and streams what it does; the caller only reads the outcome |\nsrc/docs/GOAL.md:28: | 6 | **The agent loop and planning** | Streaming tool loop, 10 rounds per message; at the limit the tools stay on the request with \u0060tool_choice: none\u0060 (FX1); a round\u0027s tool calls run at once where every toolset is read-only (F34, ADR 0026) | Stop conditions beyond a round count: budgets that warn, ask or stop (F7, Phase 5) and rounds per role and per session (F10, *Later*); Anthropic\u0027s workflow patterns where they fit: routing in code with an agent for the exceptions, evaluator-optimizer with code as the evaluator, contradicting decisions in one reply refused (F39, Phase 6, taking in F17) | Explicit planning and a self-check before answering, tried on a simulation first |\nsrc/docs/README.md:21: | [How a turn works](architecture/06-runtime-view.md) | Explanation | Follow one message through the agent: tools vs skills, the tool loop, guards, what each host shows |\nsrc/docs/SETUP.md:79: | \u0060STREAM_PACE\u0060 | \u0060on\u0060 paces a turn\u0027s text on its way out of the engine: a steady pace, in whole words, instead of the model\u0027s bursts, for every reader of that host (the API, Rider, the CLI); off by default. The model call, the tool loop, the usage records and the history run as fast as without it. With it on, the API answers each turn with \u0060Stream-Paced: on\u0060, and the framework\u0027s web leaves its own pacer off for that turn, so the words are paced once. louis-agent.dev has it on, on the PC and in Azure |\nsrc/docs/adr/0030-build-or-adopt.md:17: | Semantic Kernel | Plugins, planners, connectors; succeeded by Agent Framework | MIT | Tool loop and prompts |\nsrc/docs/adr/0064-observability-through-opentelemetry.md:10: had put Microsoft.Extensions.AI\u0027s spans in the tool loop (a \u0060chat\u0060 span per model request, \u0060execute_tool\u0060 per tool\nsrc/docs/adr/0079-show-a-specialists-report.md:24: answer from \u0060StartSubSession\u0060 or \u0060ContinueSubSession\u0060 stops the tool loop (\u0060FunctionInvocationContext.Terminate\u0060), so no\nsrc/docs/architecture/04-solution-strategy.md:9: tool loop, so the provider is configuration and the tools run in our containers\nsrc/docs/features/F09-context-management.md:54: Context is managed only at the boundaries of a turn and before \u0022continue\u0022 messages, never inside the tool loop of one\nsrc/docs/features/F16-summariser.md:71: \u0060NoPromptCaching\u0060 and its own usage recorder (as summaries are recorded today, outside the tool loop).\nsrc/docs/fixes/README.md:15: | FX1 | Keep the tools on the request after the 10-round limit | done | core: tool loop | \u0060Invalid \\\u0060signature\\\u0060 in \\\u0060thinking\\\u0060 block. The block is bound to a different conversation. \u2026 The \\\u0060tools\\\u0060 list differs from the one this block was created with.\u0060 (short demo on Haiku 5.5, 2 of 4 fixes, 2026-10-10) | After the 10th tool round the loop sent its last request without the tools, which changes what Haiku 5.5\u0027s thinking blocks are bound to | \u0060d9c7c45\u0060 | A message that uses all 10 tool rounds fails on Haiku 5.5 |\nsrc/docs/fixes/README.md:28: | FX14 | A sub-session\u0027s prompt has a budget (80k tokens, under the 100k price step): past it the specialist answers from what it has | done | core: tool loop | A specialist\u0027s prompt grew 7k \u2192 298k tokens over ten requests, and one hit the 16,000-token output limit (2026-10-14) | Every tool result is sent again on every request, past 100k Haiku 5.5 prices the whole request at five times the rate, and only the round limit bounded it | \u2014 | \u2014 |\nsrc/docs/guides/change-the-engine.md:8: ![The engine at a glance: LouisAgent.Core holds the tool loop, prompts, settings, providers and guards, and the golden files pin what the model reads](diagrams/change-the-engine.svg)\nsrc/docs/guides/change-the-engine.md:10: Core is the engine. It holds the tool loop (\u0060Agent/\u0060), asking other agents (\u0060Delegation/\u0060), skills and prompts\nsrc/docs/learning/13-cost-and-token-economics.md:52: The tool loop multiplies everything. A turn isn\u0027t one request: each tool round is one, so a fix that uses its 10\nsrc/docs/learning/13-cost-and-token-economics.md:67: **1. Record at the request, inside the tool loop (F1).** \u0060UsageRecordingChatClient\u0060 is middleware inside\nsrc/docs/learning/13-cost-and-token-economics.md:91: builds one, would need the price table, and pricing could never be added or changed without touching the tool loop.\nsrc/docs/learning/17-observability.md:36: | Spans | F18 had put Microsoft.Extensions.AI\u0027s \u0060chat\u0060 and \u0060execute_tool\u0060 spans in the tool loop, but nothing listened: they died in the process | Exported by the OpenTelemetry SDK; a turn is \u0060invoke_agent {agent}\u0060 above them, tagged with session, host, turn, run and task, never a word of the conversation |"